## Product rules that shape code
{{PRODUCT_RULES}}

## Non-negotiables
1. **Least code.** The best code is the code never written. Walk the reuse ladder: don't build → existing code →
   built-in → mature package → managed service → new code. Build only what the acceptance criteria require: no
   features, refactors or "improvements" beyond the ask, no helpers for one-off operations, no abstraction without two
   real callers, no designing for hypothetical needs. Delete dead or unwired code in the same task.
2. **IMPORTANT: tests are the spec.** The tester writes failing tests first (RED); they are locked at the RED commit. <!-- if:full -->
2. **IMPORTANT: tests are the spec.** Failing tests come first (RED), and once committed they are locked. <!-- if:lite -->
   Never weaken, skip, delete or re-mock a test and never hard-code values for test inputs. A test you believe is
   wrong becomes `TEST-DISPUTE: <test> | <AC-n> | evidence | proposed change` for the user to decide.
   Details: `.claude/rules/tests.md`.
3. **Green means green.** Compare the set of failing test IDs with the baseline, never the counts. A red baseline
   is never normal.
4. **Architecture.** Follow `docs/architecture/ARCHITECTURE.md` › Principles and the accepted ADRs. Folders are named
   by what they are; no `misc/` or `helpers/` dumping grounds. Changing a principle takes an ADR the user approves.
5. **File size.** Split by responsibility before a file reaches 500 lines. Shrinking is always allowed.
   `guard.py` blocks growth above 1000 lines (CLAUDE.md 200, task files 300, issue files 150). <!-- if:guard -->
6. **Your knowledge is stale.** Before using a third-party API, CLI flag or config key not yet verified in this repo:
   installed version → current official docs (Context7 or the vendor's site); record URL, version and date in the
   spec or report. Registry-check every new package. Never keep a deprecated API "for consistency".
7. **Canonical locations only** (table below). No summary, notes or report files anywhere else.
8. **Docs describe the current state.** Update them in the same task, in place, without dated "update" blocks;
   history lives in git and `tasks/`.
9. **The user decides.** Ask when unsure; never guess. For stack and architecture, bring 2–3 options, least code
   first, with evidence; the user picks; record an ADR from `docs/architecture/decisions/0000-template.md`.
   Never claim a task is done before the user has confirmed it.
10. **No fallbacks.** Fail loudly with full diagnostics: no silent fallbacks, default values or swallowed errors.
    Sample data only through an explicit, visibly labelled mode, never as a silent substitute when a real source fails.
11. **What counts as an issue:** a defect found outside the current task's RED tests, or one that needed ≥ 2
    debugging attempts. Record it in `docs/issues/`: search the catalog first, one record per problem.
    The `managing-known-issues` skill has the steps. <!-- if:skills -->
12. **Only the 6 roles write**, one at a time, orchestrated by `/task`: planner, tester, developer, debugger, <!-- if:full -->
    reviewer, doc-keeper. Any other subagent is read-only in this project. <!-- if:full -->

